# Deployment Instructions

## Prerequisites

- `kubectl` installed and configured to connect to your cluster
- The `mateapp` namespace exists in the cluster
- The `todoapp` ClusterIP service is running in the `mateapp` namespace

### Create the namespace (if it doesn't exist)

```bash
kubectl create namespace mateapp
```

---

## Deploy DaemonSet

Apply the DaemonSet manifest:

```bash
kubectl apply -f daemonset.yml
```

Verify the DaemonSet is running on all nodes:

```bash
kubectl get daemonset -n mateapp
```

---

## Deploy CronJob

Apply the CronJob manifest:

```bash
kubectl apply -f cronjob.yml
```

Verify the CronJob was created:

```bash
kubectl get cronjob -n mateapp
```

---

## Validation

### Validate DaemonSet

Check that a pod is scheduled on every node:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n mateapp -l app=todoapp-curl-daemonset -o wide
```

View logs from a DaemonSet pod to confirm `curl` is executing every 5 seconds:

```bash
# Get the pod name
kubectl get pods -n mateapp -l app=todoapp-curl-daemonset

# Stream logs from one of the pods
kubectl logs -n mateapp -l app=todoapp-curl-daemonset -f
```

Expected output: repeated curl responses from the todoapp every ~5 seconds.

---

### Validate CronJob

Check the CronJob status and last schedule time:

```bash
kubectl get cronjob -n mateapp todoapp-health-cronjob
```

List Jobs created by the CronJob:

```bash
kubectl get jobs -n mateapp -l app=todoapp-health-cronjob
```

View logs from the most recently completed CronJob pod:

```bash
# Get completed pod name
kubectl get pods -n mateapp -l app=todoapp-health-cronjob

# View logs
kubectl logs -n mateapp -l app=todoapp-health-cronjob
```

Expected output: a response from `/api/health` endpoint of the todoapp (e.g. `{"status":"ok"}`).

Check history limits (10 successful, 5 failed) are respected:

```bash
kubectl get jobs -n mateapp
```